On 28 Aug 1993 18:55 PDT, rankin@eql.caltech.edu (Pat Rankin) said:
[...]
>      Both of these are perfect examples of why NetHack deletes out of date
> bones and save files.  Adding or removing either the MAIL option or the
> SCORE_ON_BOTL option ends up causing changes to internal data structures,
> rendering old data stored in the files into gibberish.  Feeding garbage
> to the program just provokes a variety of failures, closely followed by
> spurious bug reports.
> 
>      Moving/renaming out of date files instead of deleting them
> might be reasonable in some cases, particularly unreliable system
> clocks, but it won't solve the underlying problem.

Which problem are you referring to?
The problem of losing save files, or the problem of attempting to use
save files that are no longer valid?

In any case, how can moving/renaming out-of-date files hurt?
They do take up disk space, but since they are easy to identify by
their names, and since the user is informed when they are renamed,
removing them is not an onerous task.

>  People who
> choose to reconfigure and recompile without also installing a whole
> new playground directory are expected to know what they're doing,
> and `make update' under Unix does automatically touch the date of
> any existing save files.

This doesn't work for gzipped files.  (Gzip is one of the suggested
ways of compressing save files, and it's what I'm using.)  Touching a
gzipped file doesn't affect the "internal" timestamp, i.e. the
timestamp the file will have when it is ungzipped.  And nethack checks
the timestamp *after* uncompressing the file.

Maybe I should submit an independent bug report for this fact.

